Node.js’s built-in `http` module supports Unix domain sockets through `socketPath`. Use `socketPath` instead of `host` and `port`:

```js
import http from 'node:http';

const req = http.request({
  socketPath: '/tmp/app.socket',
  method: 'GET',
  path: '/',
}, (res) => {
  let body = '';

  res.on('data', chunk => {
    body += chunk;
  });

  res.on('end', () => {
    console.log(res.statusCode, body);
  });
});

req.on('error', console.error);
req.end();
```

The Node.js HTTP options specify that `socketPath` is a Unix Domain Socket and that it should be used instead of `host` and `port`.

A Node.js HTTP server can listen on the same type of socket:

```js
import http from 'node:http';

http.createServer((req, res) => {
  res.end('OK\n');
}).listen('/tmp/app.socket');
```

If a reverse proxy receives `502 Bad Gateway` or `503 Service Unavailable`, check that the proxy process has permission to access the socket and its containing directory.
